Z||000016||RossT||12-15-2000||06:10 AM||||Thanks to all..  I have decided to look for a set of 15x6 and 15x7 to refinish, rather than 15x7 on all fours.<P>Thanks Raydar and Black.. etc..  your imput is appreciated.||||reg||1
Z||000017||Raydar||12-15-2000||07:45 PM||||Ross,<P>Sorry this took so long. Been cobbling a new 'puter together.<BR>Here is the pic I promised. This is an 88 GT. I believe he has the 15x7 wheels on the front.<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/afiero1.jpg"> <P>Hope this is useful.<P>raydar<P>||||reg||11
Z||000018||Formula88||04-25-2001||10:16 AM||||Well, the '88 front suspension is wider than previous years, that's the reason for the different wheels.  But why they went with 6" rim instead of just changing the offset on 7" rims is a mystery to me.  Sure does make putting bigger tires on a PITA.||||reg||1
Z||000019||Wannabe||04-25-2001||11:26 AM||||Sounds like there are lots of people looking for a solution to this litle puzzle. The article originally quoted that said rear offset was 41mm also said that offset on the 6" rims is 38mm. For most purposes, I'd say that 3mm is insignificant, but if you want to go to 7" rims on the front, you'd need around a 48mm offset for them to not stick out. The aftermarket seems to prefer offsets between 35 - 42mm, probably the best compromise for most applications. The bigger offsets are avialable if you spend big bucks. Anybody have some suggestions for 16" or 17" rims with more offset and not too pricey?||||reg||1
Z||000020||Rob Durham||04-25-2001||03:01 PM||||Hi, this seems like a cool forum, do you guys take well to newbee's??<P>I just got an 87 GT fastback and am looking for wheels and tires, can a 16" or 17" wheel be used if so what is the widest I could go and what would the offset need to be, I want something that will be extreamly low profile but compleatly fill up the wheel opening.<P>Thanks in advance,<BR>Rob||||reg||7
Z||000021||Phaeton||04-25-2001||11:36 PM||||I measured the front and rears on my 88GT and the rears have 1/4" (6mm) more offset than the fronts.<P>Running less offset in the rear caused pronounced handling problems.  The fronts don't seem to care near as much about wrong offset (other than "hunting").||||reg||12
Z||000022||Eric||04-26-2001||12:10 AM||||I think some of you are getting backset and offset confused.  Offset is the measurement from the centerline of the wheel to the hub mounting surface.  Backset is the measurement from the inside lip of the rim and the hub mounting surface.  Forgive me if this has already been addressed; I just skimmed the thread.<P>Rob, newbees are always welcome.  There are a bunch of guys with aftermarket 17" wheels on 84-87 cars.  Just do a search on "wheel offset" you should be able to find something.||||reg||1
Z||000023||99BlackZ||04-26-2001||09:25 PM||||If you have a 7" wheel, then it is actually 8" wide because of the lips on each side.<BR>The 7 inches is the measurement of where the tire is inside those lips.<P>As stated above but with a little more detail:<P>Offset - Usually measured in millimeters.  The distance from center of rim.  Usually +35mm - +40mm. The positive offset rims are for front wheel drive usually. (Fieros have positive offset)  The less offset you have the more the wheel will stick out.<P>Backspace - the distance from the mounting point of the wheel to the inside lip of the wheel.  Usually measured in inches.<P>So a 15X7" wheel with a +35mm offset will have a back space of   5.378"<P>Class dismissed.||||reg||1
Z||000024||Phaeton||04-27-2001||12:08 AM||||Assbackwards again!  I hate when I do that.<P>Not to worry though, I'm gonna guess on the next unfamiliar term still.||||reg||12
Z||000025||LZeitgeist||04-27-2001||08:20 AM||||Man, if you guys want to see something that's *totally* screwed-up-looking, put 215/60R15 tires on the 15x6" wheels, and 205/60R15 tires on the 15x7" wheels, and put the 15x7's on the front of an '88, and the 15x6's on the rear...<P>That's the way we found Formula88's Formula... evidently the previous owner didn't have a clue, or his tire shop was run by idiots...<P>Cost 80 bucks just to get the wheels and tires swapped around to the proper places on the car...||||reg||1
Z||000026||qwikgta||04-27-2001||06:10 PM||||More pics of an 88 with 87 GT wheels on all four corners see my site below.  <BR>  ?? for you all.  I was going to get a set of 16" Baretta GT wheels.  They are 16x7 I think, i think i will have the same prob, anyone done this..again its an 88 coupe.<P>Rob<P>------------------<BR>qwikgta@yahoo.com<BR>88 T-Top coupe<BR>http://robsfieroproject.homestead.com/index.html<BR>http://vafiero.com||||reg||1
Z||000027||Master_Sushi||04-27-2001||07:05 PM||||I have a question. If I used a pair of 86-87 lace wheels on my 88 coupe. Then got a thin pair of spacers (I dono what size) and put them on the back wheels would it look OK? Would it screw up handeling at all either? Also, im pretty sure the 14inch wheels were the same for all years. on my 88 coupe I can see the front wheels stick out a bit, not as much as it seems with 15's though.||||reg||5
Z||000028||Phaeton||04-27-2001||08:29 PM||||qwikgta--I am running a set of bright red Beretta GT wheels.  The backspacing is off and the Fiero handled very poorly until a set of longer wheel studs and a 1/2" spacer was put on the rear.<P>They do look fantastic, and the tendency for the front to wander could be fixed by running narrower rubber on the front wheels, at least I was told that too wide was the problem.<P>Oh yeah, the wheels are 16x7 and have 225-50 tires.||||reg||12
Z||000029||Ditz88GT||06-12-2001||10:59 AM||||I know this is an older post, but I have one more question.  I have an 88 GT and I wanted to get after market wheels.  Would I have to play around with all of this confusing off set stuff (I'm not the sharpest Crayon in the box)?  Or could I use a spacer?  If I could use the spacer, where do you buy them?<P>Ditz88GT<BR> <A HREF="http://go.to/fiero" TARGET=_blank>http://go.to/fiero</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000030||Formula88||06-12-2001||02:06 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by LZeitgeist:<BR><B>Man, if you guys want to see something that's *totally* screwed-up-looking, put 215/60R15 tires on the 15x6" wheels, and 205/60R15 tires on the 15x7" wheels, and put the 15x7's on the front of an '88, and the 15x6's on the rear...<P>That's the way we found Formula88's Formula... evidently the previous owner didn't have a clue, or his tire shop was run by idiots...<P>Cost 80 bucks just to get the wheels and tires swapped around to the proper places on the car...</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Hey, *I* was gonna tell them that.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>Man, you want to talk about squirrelly?  The back end wanted to come around in almost any corner.  I guess the front wheels were just anxious to lead the way.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Oh, and someone asked about the 14" wheels in 88.  They are all the same.  LZeitgeist had an '88 coupe with the stock rims.  Yup, they do stick out slightly in front, but it's not as noticeable on the 14" rims as it would be with 15x7's on the front.||||reg||1
Z||000031||Mickey_Moose||09-03-2001||11:47 AM||||old thread, but I have to set the record straight...the yellow 88 posted above, looks like mine (that where is was sitting in the driveway when the picture was taken, looks like the neighbour accros the way too) - anyways, it is an 88 GT with correct tires sizes on the correct original rims (mounted correctly)<P>anyways<BR>Tim<BR>||||reg||1
